+# Why production requests land on `mainnet.base.org`
+
+## TL;DR
+
+`NEXT_PUBLIC_RPC_URL` is **inlined at build time**, not read at runtime. The
+production static bundle was built in an environment where that variable was
+**not set**, so `process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_RPC_URL` was frozen into the client JS
+as `undefined`. With the override missing, SE-2's transport falls back to a bare
+`http()`, and viem's bare `http()` resolves to the chain's **default public RPC**
+— for Base that is `https://mainnet.base.org`. Hence the 429s. Dev machines have
+the variable in `.env.local`, so their build inlines the paid URL and never
+touches the public endpoint.
+
+---
+
+## The exact chain
+
+### 1. `scaffold.config.ts` reads the env var
+```ts
+rpcOverrides: {
+  [chains.base.id]: process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_RPC_URL,
+},
+```
+This is not "read the RPC URL." It's "read the value of `process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_RPC_URL`
+**at the moment the code is compiled**."
+
+### 2. Next.js inlines `NEXT_PUBLIC_*` at build time
+Any identifier `process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_FOO` in code that ends up in the browser
+bundle is **textually substituted** by Next.js's webpack `DefinePlugin` during
+`next build`. It is not a runtime lookup. Two consequences:
+
+- Only `NEXT_PUBLIC_`-prefixed vars are exposed to the browser at all.
+- The value is **whatever was in the shell/`.env` when `next build` ran**, baked
+  in as a string literal. If the var is absent at build time, the substitution
+  is literally `undefined`.
+
+So after build, `rpcOverrides[base.id]` is a **constant** in the shipped JS —
+either `"https://paid-rpc..."` or `undefined`. Nothing on the server can change
+it later.
+
+### 3. Dev vs. production diverge here
+- **Dev machines**: `.env.local` (or `.env`) contains `NEXT_PUBLIC_RPC_URL=<paid>`.
+  `next dev`/`next build` inlines the paid URL → override present → paid RPC. No 429s.
+- **Production static build**: the deploy is a **static Next.js build on generic
+  hosting**. The var was almost certainly **not present in the build/CI
+  environment** (or was set only in the hosting platform's *runtime* env, which a
+  static build never reads). It inlines as `undefined`. Setting the variable in
+  the host's runtime dashboard does nothing — there is no server process
+  re-reading env for `NEXT_PUBLIC_*`; the value is already frozen in the static
+  chunks.
+
+### 4. `wagmiConfig.tsx` transport with the override missing
+```ts
+client({ chain }) {
+  let rpcFallbacks = [http()];                                   // <-- default
+
+  const rpcOverrideUrl = scaffoldConfig.rpcOverrides?.[chain.id];
+  if (rpcOverrideUrl) {
+    rpcFallbacks = [http(rpcOverrideUrl), http()];
+  } else {
+    // alchemy default-key branch, still ends with a bare http()
+  }
+
+  return createClient({ chain, transport: fallback(rpcFallbacks, { rank: false }), ... });
+}
+```
+With `rpcOverrideUrl === undefined`, the `if` is skipped and the base client
+keeps `rpcFallbacks = [http()]`.
+
+### 5. What a bare `http()` does
+`http(url?)` with **no URL** does not throw and does not disable the chain. viem
+falls back to `chain.rpcUrls.default.http[0]`. For viem's `base` chain that is
+**`https://mainnet.base.org`** — the public endpoint. Under production traffic
+that endpoint rate-limits → **429**.
+
+That is the whole loop: missing build-time var → `undefined` override →
+`http()` with no arg → viem's Base default public RPC → 429.
+
+---
+
+## The one operational check that settles whether the fix is real
+
+**Inspect the actual RPC host in the shipped artifact, not the config or the
+hosting dashboard.** Concretely, either:
+
+- Open the production site, DevTools → **Network**, and confirm the JSON-RPC
+  POSTs go to the **paid host**, not `mainnet.base.org`; or equivalently
+- `grep -r "mainnet.base.org\|<paid-rpc-host>" .next/static` (or the deployed
+  `_next/static` chunks) and confirm the **paid URL is baked in** and the public
+  one is not the one being called.
+
+Why this and nothing else: because the value is inlined at build time, the only
+proof that the fix works is that the **paid URL is present in the deployed client
+bundle / actual live requests**. Changing `.env`, setting the var in the host
+dashboard, or "it's set in CI now" prove nothing until you **rebuild and
+redeploy** and see the paid host in the bundle/Network tab. The fix is real iff
+the shipped JS calls the paid RPC.
+
+---
+
+## The second, transport-level trap (persists even after the env var is fixed)
+
+Look at the override branch itself:
+```ts
+rpcFallbacks = [http(rpcOverrideUrl), http()];
+//                                    ^^^^^^  bare http() = public RPC
+```
+Even once the paid URL is correctly inlined, the transport is a viem
+`fallback([ paid, public ])`. The **second entry is a bare `http()`**, i.e. the
+same public `mainnet.base.org`. viem's `fallback` transport routes to the next
+transport whenever the current one **errors** — including on timeouts, 5xx, and
+crucially **429 rate-limit responses**. So under load, or any hiccup on the paid
+endpoint, a share of requests **still spill onto the public RPC**, which can then
+429 in turn. The public endpoint is never removed from the routing set; it's
+demoted to failover, not eliminated.
+
+(Related, same root cause: SE-2 always appends `mainnet` to `enabledChains` for
+ENS/price lookups, and `rpcOverrides` only covers `base.id`. The mainnet client
+has no override, so its `rpcFallbacks` also ends in a bare `http()` → public
+mainnet RPC. Different host, same "bare `http()` = public" trap.)
+
+**To actually stop public-RPC traffic**, drop the trailing bare `http()` from the
+fallback array (or replace it with a second *paid/keyed* endpoint), and provide
+an override for every enabled chain — including the implicitly-added mainnet —
+rather than leaving a public endpoint in the transport at all.

